@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Subquery with SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE & DELETE";
}

<div class="container-fluid">

    <!-- =========================
         PAGE HEADER
    ========================== -->

    <div class="mb-4">

        <h1 class="fw-bold">
            Subquery with SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE &amp; DELETE
        </h1>

        <p class="text-muted">
            Learn how subqueries can be used with SELECT, INSERT,
            UPDATE and DELETE statements.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         INTRODUCTION
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Introduction
            </h2>

            <p>
                A subquery can be used not only with SELECT statements,
                but also with <strong>INSERT</strong>,
                <strong>UPDATE</strong> and <strong>DELETE</strong>
                statements.
            </p>

            <p>
                The subquery provides the required data or condition
                that is used by the main SQL statement.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         SELECT WITH SUBQUERY
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                1. SELECT with Subquery
            </h2>

            <p>
                A subquery can be used inside a SELECT statement to
                filter records based on the result of another query.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>
SELECT EmployeeName, Salary
FROM Employees
WHERE Salary &gt;
(
    SELECT AVG(Salary)
    FROM Employees
);
            </code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                The inner query calculates the average salary and the
                outer query returns employees whose salary is greater
                than the average.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         INSERT WITH SUBQUERY
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                2. INSERT with Subquery
            </h2>

            <p>
                A subquery can be used with INSERT to insert the result
                of a SELECT query into another table.
            </p>

            <p>
                Suppose we have an archive table:
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>
CREATE TABLE EmployeeArchive
(
    EmployeeId INT,
    EmployeeName VARCHAR(100),
    Salary DECIMAL(10,2)
);
            </code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                We can insert selected employees using a subquery:
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>
INSERT INTO EmployeeArchive
    (EmployeeId, EmployeeName, Salary)
SELECT EmployeeId, EmployeeName, Salary
FROM Employees
WHERE DepartmentId IN
(
    SELECT DepartmentId
    FROM Departments
    WHERE Location = 'Pune'
);
            </code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                The inner query finds departments located in Pune.
                The SELECT query then gets employees belonging to those
                departments and inserts them into
                <strong>EmployeeArchive</strong>.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         UPDATE WITH SUBQUERY
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                3. UPDATE with Subquery
            </h2>

            <p>
                A subquery can be used in an UPDATE statement to
                determine which records should be updated.
            </p>

            <p>
                Example: Increase the salary of employees who belong
                to the IT department.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>
UPDATE Employees
SET Salary = Salary + 5000
WHERE DepartmentId =
(
    SELECT DepartmentId
    FROM Departments
    WHERE DepartmentName = 'IT'
);
            </code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                The subquery finds the department ID of the IT
                department. The UPDATE statement then increases the
                salary of employees belonging to that department.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         DELETE WITH SUBQUERY
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                4. DELETE with Subquery
            </h2>

            <p>
                A subquery can also be used with DELETE to identify
                the records that should be removed.
            </p>

            <p>
                Example: Delete employees who belong to a department
                located in Mumbai.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>
DELETE FROM Employees
WHERE DepartmentId IN
(
    SELECT DepartmentId
    FROM Departments
    WHERE Location = 'Mumbai'
);
            </code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                The inner query finds the departments located in
                Mumbai. The DELETE statement removes employees
                belonging to those departments.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         COMPARISON TABLE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Subquery with SQL Statements
            </h2>

            <div class="table-responsive">

                <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                    <thead class="table-primary">

                        <tr>
                            <th>Statement</th>
                            <th>Purpose</th>
                            <th>Example Use</th>
                        </tr>

                    </thead>

                    <tbody>

                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>SELECT</strong></td>
                            <td>
                                Retrieve records based on another query.
                            </td>
                            <td>
                                Find employees above average salary.
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>INSERT</strong></td>
                            <td>
                                Insert data obtained from another query.
                            </td>
                            <td>
                                Copy selected employees to an archive.
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>UPDATE</strong></td>
                            <td>
                                Update records selected using a subquery.
                            </td>
                            <td>
                                Update employees of a particular department.
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>DELETE</strong></td>
                            <td>
                                Delete records identified by a subquery.
                            </td>
                            <td>
                                Delete employees from a particular location.
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                    </tbody>

                </table>

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         IMPORTANT POINTS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="alert alert-warning mb-4">

        <h4 class="fw-bold">
            Important Points
        </h4>

        <ul class="mb-0">

            <li>
                A subquery can be used with SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE
                and DELETE.
            </li>

            <li>
                Always verify the result of the subquery before
                performing UPDATE or DELETE operations.
            </li>

            <li>
                INSERT ... SELECT is useful for copying selected
                records between tables.
            </li>

            <li>
                UPDATE with a subquery can be used to modify records
                based on related data.
            </li>

            <li>
                DELETE with a subquery can remove records based on
                conditions from another table.
            </li>

            <li>
                Be especially careful with UPDATE and DELETE because
                they can modify or remove multiple records.
            </li>

        </ul>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         SUMMARY
    ========================== -->

    <div class="alert alert-info p-4 mb-4">

        <h2 class="fw-bold">
            Summary
        </h2>

        <p class="mb-0">
            Subqueries can be used with SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE and
            DELETE statements. They allow one query to provide data
            or conditions for another SQL operation and are useful
            when working with related data across tables.
        </p>

    </div>


